Vendored from crates.io ndarray-npy 0.9.1, registry checksum
7b313788c468c49141a9d9b6131fc15f403e6ef4e8446a0b2e18f664ddb278a9 as recorded in
the workspace Cargo.lock before this change.

Upstream: https://github.com/jturner314/ndarray-npy

Modified by CCR-Rust:
- removed the `zip` dependency and the `npz` / `compressed_npz` features, and
  deleted the `src/npz.rs` module they gated, its `mod` / `pub use` lines, and
  the now-dangling `.npz` intra-doc links;
- removed the `simple_npz` example declaration, which required the removed
  feature;
- left every remaining source file byte-identical to the published crate.

Why: every published egobox-gp release through 0.38.0 pins `ndarray-npy ^0.9`,
whose default `compressed_npz` feature pulls in `zip 2.4.2`. That version has
three open Dependabot advisories, all patched only in `zip 9.0.5`, and no
`cargo update` path exists because `ndarray-npy 0.11.0` is the first release
accepting `zip ^9`. Neither this workspace nor egobox-gp performs any `.npz`
I/O, so the feature is dead weight. Dropping it removes `zip`, `zopfli`, and
`arbitrary` from the graph and closes the advisories without touching the
`.npy` read and write paths that egobox-gp actually uses.

Reopen condition: replace this vendored crate with the registry release once
egobox-gp publishes a version requiring `ndarray-npy >= 0.11`, and then delete
the `[patch.crates-io]` entry in the workspace root Cargo.toml.

`vendor/gp-routing/Cargo.toml` carries the same `[patch.crates-io]` entry so the
standalone `cargo test --manifest-path vendor/gp-routing/Cargo.toml --locked`
quality check resolves to this crate too, and its Cargo.lock drops `zip`,
`zopfli`, `flate2`, `miniz_oxide`, `simd-adler32`, `derive_arbitrary`, and
`displaydoc` with no other version movement. Without that entry the standalone
build would still compile the vulnerable `zip` release from its own lock.
